  "adaptations": [
    "Panel boundaries and control centres follow the supplied 1586 by 992 Obsidian reference. All controls and plots are native and remain live; no reference bitmap is used as the instrument UI.",
    "The header keeps working preset navigation, scene selection, skin selection, full-editor access and real master volume. Duplicate scene and voice controls have been removed from the otherwise empty lower reference margin; the original editor retains them.",
    "Oscillator power glyphs operate the actual mute parameters. Solo and mute remain available in the mixer. Algorithms, parameter names and values come from Surge; the generic concept Sample label is never fabricated.",
    "The three compact table strips browse the actual installed wavetable catalog for a wavetable-capable oscillator. OCT and TUNE use the native octave and pitch parameters; independent semitone and fine parameters are not invented.",
    "The envelope strip selects ENV 1, ENV 2, LFO 1 envelope and LFO 2 envelope, rebinding the same A/D/S/R fields to their real sources. Surge has two dedicated envelope generators; the two extra sources are accurately named LFO envelopes.",
    "LFO 1 through LFO 4 tabs select the actual source preview and rebind shape, rate, fade-in attack, phase, polarity and trigger mode. The full editor retains tempo-sync and all additional LFO parameters.",
    "Per-filter envelope depth is labelled ENV instead of pretending to be an independent filter drive. Native shared waveshaper drive is retained in the full editor. Filter routing remains a schematic of actual native modes.",
    "The eight FX cards select real native slots. The algorithm menu and four detail knobs rebind to the selected slot; labels and values always come from its actual current algorithm. The first slot exposes parameter indices 4, 1, 5 and 10, which are Drive, pre-EQ frequency, Feedback and Output for the showcased Distortion; other slots expose indices 0 through 3. All sixteen slots and every parameter remain in the full editor.",
    "The FX inset is a decorative algorithm-category pictogram, not a fabricated transfer response. Output spectrum, meters, wavetable frames and envelope traces use current processor data.",
    "Macro values stay accessible and available in hover descriptions while the compact face-and-name treatment matches the reference density. The actual assigned macro names remain authoritative."
  ]
}
